---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/site";

/** ★★ 소개 글 — 자매 사이트 Field Pro(pro.radiation-lab.com)가 계산하는 것(2026-10-25 D6, 감독 지시 14 ⑧).
 *  ★ 정확도 수치는 Field Pro 저장소의 `docs/accuracy-spec.md`(엔진에서 자동 생성, `accuracy-spec.test.ts` 가 봉투를
 *    강제)에서 **편차 백분율만** 옮긴다. ISO 4037 표값(선질별 HVL·변환계수)은 저작권이 있으므로 옮기지 않는다.
 *  ★ 화면 이름은 Field Pro 의 한글 표기를 그대로 적고 영어 뜻을 붙인다 — 한글 UI 를 처음 보는 독자가 찾게. */
const PRO = "https://pro.radiation-lab.com/";
const SPEC = "https://github.com/aijhmin1031-eng/radiation-field-pro/blob/master/docs/accuracy-spec.md";
---
<Base title="ISO 4037 reference fields: what Field Pro computes"
      description="What the Field Pro workbench computes for ISO 4037 reference X-ray and gamma fields: filtered tungsten spectra, half-value layers and homogeneity, matching to the 50 ISO qualities, operational dose conversion, air-density correction and measurement uncertainty, with its accuracy against the standard and a guide to its screen."
      schema="TechArticle">
  <h1 class="text-[26px] font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-[32px]">
    ISO 4037 reference fields: what Field Pro computes
  </h1>

  <section class="prose-doc mt-6 max-w-3xl text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
    <p>
      Dosimeters and survey meters are calibrated in reference radiation fields whose spectrum is known
      well enough that the conversion from air kerma to the quantity the instrument displays can be
      looked up rather than measured. The series of standards ISO 4037 defines those fields for photons:
      filtered X-ray beams for the low and middle energies, and the gamma rays of Cs-137 and Co-60 above
      them. <a href={PRO} class="underline">Field Pro</a> is the workbench on this platform for that
      setting. It models the beam a calibration laboratory actually produces, compares it with the
      quality the standard describes, and carries the result through to operational dose and its
      uncertainty. This note explains what each part computes, how accurate it is, and how its screen
      is laid out.
    </p>

    <h2>Why reference fields need a model</h2>
    <p>
      An ISO radiation quality, such as N-60 or H-100, is specified by its tube voltage, its added
      filtration and the half-value layers the beam should have. The conversion coefficients that turn
      air kerma into personal dose equivalent Hp(10) or ambient dose equivalent H*(10) depend on the
      spectrum, and the standard tabulates them for the nominal beam. A real tube is never quite the
      nominal one. Its inherent filtration differs, its anode has aged, the air path to the reference
      point is not exactly the one assumed, and laboratories build qualities that the standard does not
      list. In each case the question is the same: how far is this beam from the one the coefficients
      were computed for, and does the difference matter at the accuracy the calibration claims? A model
      gives a first answer in seconds and shows which input moves the result most.
    </p>

    <h2>The X-ray spectrum</h2>
    <p>
      Field Pro computes the spectrum of a tungsten anode at a 20° angle for tube voltages from 10 to
      400&nbsp;kV, then passes it through a stack of filters. The filter materials are beryllium,
      aluminium, copper, tin, tantalum, tungsten and lead, entered as layers in any order and thickness,
      together with the air path from the focal spot to the point of test. Inherent filtration (the tube
      window and housing) and added filtration are separate entries, because one is fixed by the tube and
      the other is what the laboratory changes to build a quality.
    </p>
    <p>
      From the filtered spectrum it reports the mean energy, the effective energy (the energy of a single
      line with the same first half-value layer), the first and second half-value layers in aluminium,
      copper or lead, the homogeneity coefficient h&nbsp;=&nbsp;HVL1/HVL2, and the relative air kerma. The
      homogeneity coefficient summarises how hard the beam is: a beam close to one line has h near 1,
      and an unfiltered beam much less, because the first layer strips its soft end. The raw spectrum without any of this filtration is what the
      <a href={u("/xray-spectrum/")} class="underline">X-ray tube spectrum</a> tool on this site draws;
      Field Pro starts where that tool stops.
    </p>

    <h2>Matching a beam to an ISO quality</h2>
    <p>
      ISO 4037-1:2019 lists 50 qualities in four series. The narrow-spectrum series N, used for most
      dosimeter calibrations, and the low air-kerma series L have heavy filtration and high homogeneity;
      the wide series W gives more air kerma at the cost of a broader spectrum; and the high air-kerma
      series H is lightly filtered. Field Pro compares the computed beam with every quality and names the
      one whose first half-value layer, worked out in that quality's own material, is closest. If you
      choose a quality as the target instead, it sets the beam beside it characteristic by
      characteristic (HVL1, HVL2, homogeneity coefficient and mean energy) and flags each against an
      acceptance band you set, which is the usual way to check that a new filter set reproduces the
      intended beam.
    </p>
    <p>
      The comparison is only as good as the inputs. The inherent filtration of a tube is often known
      only roughly, and at low voltages it moves the half-value layer noticeably. Measuring HVL1 once on
      the real beam and adjusting the inherent filtration until the model agrees pins it down.
    </p>

    <h2>Gamma fields from Cs-137 and Co-60</h2>
    <p>
      For the nuclide fields, Field Pro takes the source activity on its reference date, corrects it for
      decay to the day of the measurement, and gives the air kerma rate at the chosen distance treating
      the source as a point. The same calculation for any nuclide, with shielding, is in the
      <a href={u("/gamma-shielding/")} class="underline">gamma shielding</a> tool, and the decay
      correction alone is in the <a href={u("/decay/")} class="underline">decay</a> tool. What Field Pro
      adds is the step from air kerma to the operational quantities with coefficients averaged over the
      source spectrum.
    </p>

    <h2>Operational dose conversion</h2>
    <p>
      Calibrations are stated in air kerma and then converted to the quantity the instrument is meant to
      show: Hp(10) for personal dosemeters worn on the body, H*(10) for area survey meters, and the
      directional dose equivalent H′(0.07) for skin and extremity dose. Field Pro averages the
      monoenergetic conversion coefficients over the computed spectrum, weighted by air kerma, and does
      so for an angle of incidence α from 0° to 75°. The angle matters for personal dosemeters, which are
      tested at several angles because their response falls away as the beam moves off the normal.
    </p>

    <h2>Correcting the reading</h2>
    <p>
      An open ionisation chamber responds to the mass of air in its volume, so its reading depends on
      the air density at the time of measurement. Field Pro computes the correction factor k<sub>ρ</sub>
      from temperature, pressure and humidity, following the reading corrections in ISO 4037-4.
    </p>

    <h2>Uncertainty</h2>
    <p>
      The workbench builds an uncertainty budget in the form of the Guide to the Expression of
      Uncertainty in Measurement (GUM). The Type A component comes from repeated readings, corrected
      first, as u&nbsp;=&nbsp;s/√n; Type B components are entered by name as relative standard
      uncertainties. They are combined as a root sum of squares, the effective degrees of freedom
      ν<sub>eff</sub> come from the Welch–Satterthwaite formula, and the expanded uncertainty is given
      with a coverage factor from the t-distribution for about 95% coverage, in a form that can go into a
      calibration certificate. Rerunning the beam model with the filtration at the ends of its plausible
      range shows how far the conversion coefficient can move, a sound basis for that Type B term.
    </p>

    <h2>How close it comes to the standard</h2>
    <p>
      The accuracy of the engine is checked against the standard by an automated test, and a
      <a href={SPEC} class="underline">published accuracy specification</a> is generated from the engine
      itself, not typed in. For each of the 50 X-ray qualities, the reference beam (inherent and added
      filtration plus 2.5&nbsp;m of air) is modelled and its first half-value layer compared with the
      value in ISO 4037-1:
    </p>
    <ul class="my-3 space-y-1">
      <li>Low energies, mean energy up to 40&nbsp;keV, 17 qualities: mean deviation 1.26%, largest 3.91%,
        against a guaranteed envelope of 4%.</li>
      <li>Middle energies, 40 to 150&nbsp;keV, 22 qualities: mean 0.67%, largest 1.45%, envelope 2%.</li>
      <li>High energies, 150&nbsp;keV and above, 11 qualities: mean 1.02%, largest 1.69%, envelope 2%.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For the gamma fields,
      the spectrum-averaged conversion coefficients for Hp(10), H*(10) and H′(0.07) at normal incidence
      agree with the values recommended in ISO 4037-3 to within 1.03% for both Cs-137 and Co-60, against
      an envelope of 2%. The specification lists the deviation for every quality but not the tabulated
      values themselves, which belong to the standard.
    </p>
    <p>
      These figures say how well the model reproduces the nominal fields. They do not replace the
      measurements a laboratory makes to characterise its own beams, and a calibration should rest on
      those.
    </p>

    <h2>Finding your way around the workbench</h2>
    <p>
      The screen is in English and uses the quantity names, symbols and units of ISO 4037 and ICRU, so
      its labels read the same as the standard. The inputs sit
      in four numbered panels in the left column and the results in lettered panels on the right. On a
      phone, the Input and Results tabs switch between the two, and the bar fixed at the bottom of the
      screen runs the calculation.
    </p>
    <ul class="my-3 space-y-1">
      <li><b>01 Source</b>: X-ray tube, Cs-137, Co-60 or Import (a measured spectrum). For a tube, the
        fields below take the ISO beam quality (choosing one fills in its tube voltage and filtration,
        and the menu reads <i>Custom (edited)</i> once you change them by hand), the tube voltage in kV, the air path in millimetres, and
        the inherent and added filtration, with <i>+ Filter layer</i> to add another layer.</li>
      <li><b>02 Geometry and evaluation</b>: the angle of incidence α, the HVL material (automatic,
        following the matched ISO quality, or Al, Cu or Pb), the ISO comparison target and the
        tolerance band in percent.</li>
      <li><b>03 Readout correction</b> and <b>04 Repeatability uncertainty</b>: temperature, pressure
        and relative humidity for k<sub>ρ</sub>, then the repeat readings for the Type A component and
        the Type B components.</li>
      <li><b>Results A to D</b>: beam-quality characteristics (Ē, E<sub>eff</sub>, HVL1, HVL2, h),
        operational dose conversion coefficients, the ISO 4037-1 reference-field comparison and the
        measurement uncertainty in report form, each with the change from the previous calculation.</li>
      <li>The toolbar under the title holds <i>Calculate</i>, an <i>Auto-calculate</i> checkbox, and
        <i>Save session</i>, <i>Load session</i>, <i>Print report</i> and <i>Reset</i>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Every calculation runs in the browser. Nothing you enter is sent to a server, and a saved session
      is a file on your own computer. The source code is public under the MIT licence.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to go next</h2>
    <ul class="my-3 space-y-1">
      <li><a href={PRO} class="underline">Field Pro</a> — the workbench itself.</li>
      <li><a href={u("/xray-spectrum/")} class="underline">X-ray tube spectrum</a> — the raw tungsten
        spectrum before filtration, with its mean energy.</li>
      <li><a href={u("/half-value-layers/")} class="underline">Half-value and tenth-value layers</a> — the
        same idea for shielding gamma emitters, and why the second layer is thicker than the first.</li>
      <li><a href={u("/gamma-shielding/")} class="underline">Gamma shielding</a> — point-source air kerma
        rate for any nuclide, with attenuation and buildup.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
